sunny beaches flashy bars romantic  waterfalls tom cruise's lowest rated  rotten tomatoes movie may typify cheesy  80s cinema but that doesn't mean it's  all bad in fact the bar themed cocktail  is actually worth watching here's why  tom cruise is one of the biggest movie  stars in the world and that's been the  case for a very long time the actor rose  to prominence in the 1980s thanks to  movies such as risky business and top  gun both of which amassed plenty of  favor among fans and critics alike that  said some of cruz's biggest hits from  his breakout decade haven't enjoyed  similar to claim cocktail released in  1988 was a financial success but the  movie was savage by critics as evidenced  by its seven percent score on rotten  tomatoes as of june 2022 it's cruz's  lowest rated movie on the review  aggregator based on haywood gould's  semi-autobiographical novel the same  name cocktail centers around brian  flanagan played by tom cruise a  bartender who dreams of becoming a  salesman on wall street unfortunately  the top sales firms only want to hire  people with experience in college  degrees so he pursues his fortune by  opening beach bars and flirting with the  gigolo lifestyle along the way he  befriends a veteran bartender called  duck played by brian brown falls in love  with a woman named jordan played by  elizabeth shu and makes some costly  mistakes it's an experience to say the  least is cocktail the best movie and  cruises body of work absolutely not but  the film is far too entertaining to be  cruz's lowest ranked movie  eyes open  cocktail is loaded with memorable  moments that typify 80s cinema at its  most excessive and ridiculous and that's  a good thing if you can appreciate a  little bit of cheese in your cinematic  diet characters juggling beer bottles at  the bar like they're performing at a  circus check bartenders performing  alcohol-themed poetry to a nightclub  full of wealthy socialites check fist  fights or art exhibits check throw in a  hip 80s soundtrack and crew singing  robert palmer is addicted to love and  what you have is a recipe for success  there's no denying that cocktail is a  cheesy flick at times however when you  strip away the allure of the sunny  beaches romantic waterfalls and flashy  cocktail bars you'll find a story about  a flawed man who undergoes an  interesting transformation when we first  meet brian he's a materialistic  womanizer who only cares about making  money which causes him to demonstrate  some self-destructive behaviors by the  end he's a changed man who values more  substantial things such as love and  family cocktail isn't particularly deep  but it doesn't gloss over some of the  warts and all elements of the  protagonist's topsy-turvy journey the  original screenplay was a complicated  story about the dark side of power and  money but when disney boarded the  project those elements were toned down  as the studio wanted the characters to  be more likable and hopeful still some  of those themes and ideas are still  apparent in the version of cocktail that  exists today doug's death for example is  particularly upsetting though it does  drive home the film's core message about  the dangers of excess and living a  vacuous empty life for a movie that was  marketed as a lighthearted rom-com  cocktail still possesses a cynical  streak furthermore the film's total  shifts flow together pretty seamlessly  which is remarkable considering that it  can shift from a ridiculous moment to a  harrowing one at any given time it'd be  unfair to say that tom cruise makes the  same movie over and over again after all  he's probably the best action movie star  in the west renowned for performing his  own risky stunts and going the extra  mile for his fans at the same time some  viewers have pointed out that cruz has  drawn toward playing specific types of  characters and they aren't wrong per se  that's not a bad thing mind you as  audiences can't get enough of him help  me  help you  in the 1980s cruz was prone to playing  ambitious underdogs who wanted to be the  best at their respective locations top  gun for example saw him portray a cocky  pilot who experiences a crisis of  conscience and falls in love his days of  thunder character experiences a similar  arc albeit from behind a race car  steering wheel instead of the cockpit of  a fighter jet cocktail has a similar  framework only set within the world of  bars and clubs cruz carries all these  movies with his trademark charm and his  performance in cocktail plays to his  strengths in short if you're a fan of  movies like top gun and days of thunder  then cocktail will be right up your  alley no matter what the critics say  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies and tv shows are  coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one
 

